/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Membrane: NF270 with PER-ION permeances for the SDEM transport law,
  the row "NaCl" of Table 1 of

      M. Fernandez de Labastida, A. Yaroshchuk, "Nanofiltration of
      Multi-Ion Solutions: Quantitative Control of Concentration
      Polarization and Interpretation by Solution-Diffusion-Electro-
      Migration Model", Membranes 11 (2021) 272,
      doi:10.3390/membranes11040272 -- CC BY 4.0.

  WHAT THESE NUMBERS ARE.  Ion permeances the authors FITTED with the SDEM
  model to their own CP-corrected (intrinsic) rejections on a flat NF270
  disc in a rotating-disk cell: dominant salt NaCl at 0.01 mol/L, trace
  salts (NaNO3, NH4Cl) at 2e-4 mol/L, 20 C, 2-14 bar.  The permeances
  DEPEND ON THE DOMINANT SALT (the paper's point: Donnan and dielectric
  exclusion set the interphase potential, and with it every ion's
  permeance), which is why this record names the salt it belongs to and a
  sibling record carries the MgSO4 row.  A value the paper gives as a bound
  or a range is marked below with the value taken and why.

  THE CHECK THE RECORD CARRIES.  Under zero current a single salt passes
  with the AMBIPOLAR permeance P_s = (z+ + |z-|) P+ P- / (z+ P+ + |z-| P-);
  the table's own salt permeance for NaCl is 7.5 um/s, and the per-ion
  values below reproduce it to the table's rounding (check_sdem recomputes
  this in Python, independently of the engine).

  A_w: the standard NF270 record's value (data/standards/assets/NF270.dat),
  NOT measured in the paper -- the rejections the witness compares are
  functions of the flux the run produces, so A_w only places the operating
  point on the paper's 2-14 bar range.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
name           NF270_sdem_NaCl;
kind           NF;
manufacturer   "DuPont FilmTec";
A_w            1.0e-5  m/s/bar;     // data/standards/assets/NF270.dat
//  PROVENANCE, machine-readable: the values are a PRIMARY SOURCE's own fit
//  (the paper's Table 1), not a fit to any Choupo case.  The citation and
//  the licence that lets a CC BY table be transcribed here:
provenance     primarySource;
source         "Fernandez de Labastida & Yaroshchuk, Membranes 11 (2021) 272, Table 1; doi:10.3390/membranes11040272";
licence        CC-BY-4.0;
permeabilities
{
    Na      1.130e-04  m/s;   // 20-113 um/s in the paper (fit insensitive above 20); 113 reproduces P_s = 7.5
    Cl      3.900e-06  m/s;
    NO3     1.380e-05  m/s;
    NH4     6.000e-05  m/s;   // paper gives '>60': the lower bound
}
P_max          41.4;            // bar (600 psi)
T_max          318;             // K (45 C)
pH_min         3;
pH_max         10;
MWCO           200;             // Da (loose NF)
